## Why This Is Asked
Understanding end-to-end performance in a service mesh with multiple microservices and chaos scenarios is critical for real-world reliability.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end tail latency across services
- Workload modeling (Poisson bursts, ramp stages)
- Backpressure and circuit breaking
- Observability (OpenTelemetry, metrics, traces)
- Chaos testing and fault injection in Istio

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export let options = { stages: [ { duration: '5m', target: 20000 } ] };
export default function () {
  const r = http.post('https://service/booking', JSON.stringify({ rider:'r1', pickup:'A', dropoff:'B' }), { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }});
  check(r, {'status is 200': (r)=> r.status === 200});
  sleep(0.5);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for geo-distributed deployments?
- What thresholds would you set for p95/p99 under varied network conditions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world concurrency and multi-region resilience for a streaming AI workload beyond typical batch jobs.

## Key Concepts
- Tail latency, autoscaling, per-tenant QoS, cache effectiveness, end-to-end tracing
- Backpressure handling, failure injection, graceful degradation
- Serverless vs. reserved compute trade-offs in a streaming path

## Code Example
```javascript
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import { check } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const url = 'wss://transcription.example.com/stream';
  const s = ws.connect(url, {}, (socket) => {
    socket.on('open', () => socket.send(JSON.stringify({type:'start'})));
    socket.on('message', (msg) => {});
  });
  check(s, { 'connected': (r) => r && r.status === 101 });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model per-tenant QoS and quotas in your workload generators?
- What metrics and thresholds would you use to trigger autoscaling up and down?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design a comprehensive performance test strategy for AI inference systems operating under multi-tenant workloads with GPU autoscaling and production rollout risks.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant isolation and quota enforcement
- GPU memory pressure handling and dynamic batch sizing
- Backpressure and queueing behavior under burst loads
- Rollback and feature-flag safety mechanisms during rollouts

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo workload generator sketch
const config = {
  tenants: 10,
  qps: 25000,
  batchSizes: [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32],
  duration: '30m',
  rampUp: '5m'
};

// Track latency percentiles
function trackLatency(tenant, latencyMs) {
  percentiles.tenant[tenant].push(latencyMs);
}

// Monitor GPU pressure
gpuMetrics.on('memoryPressure', (utilization) => {
  if (utilization > 0.9) triggerAlert();
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design end-to-end performance tests spanning multiple subsystems with realistic data flows, backpressure, and failure modes; probes tail latency budgets during promos and how caching, storage, and batch layers interact under stress.

## Key Concepts

- Tail latency
- End-to-end tracing across Redis, Cassandra, embeddings, Spark
- Workload modeling: read/write mix, skew, burstiness
- Backpressure mechanisms: queues, circuit breakers, autoscaling
- Observability: metrics, logs, distributed tracing
- Failure modes: eviction, timeouts, model cold-start

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-load test skeleton (k6)
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  http.get('https://svc.example/recommend?user_id=' + Math.floor(Math.random()*100000));
  sleep(0.05);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate that cache warmth is not masking data skew?
- What monitoring dashboards and alerting thresholds would you set for p99 across components?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests realism and the ability to validate performance across regions, with realistic traffic mix, TLS/HTTP2 behavior, edge caching, and backpressure.

## Key Concepts

- Global traffic modeling across regions
- TLS handshake cost and HTTP/2 behavior
- Edge caching effectiveness
- Backpressure, queues, and circuit breakers
- Observability with OpenTelemetry, SLI/SLOs
- Soak testing and autoscaling validation

## Code Example

```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep } from 'k6';
const regions = [
  'https://us-east.example.com',
  'https://eu-west.example.com',
  'https://apac.example.com'
];
export let options = {
  scenarios: {
    regional: { executor: 'shared-iterations', vus: 1000, iterations: 10000 }
  }
};
export function api() {
  http.get(regions[Math.floor(Math.random() * regions.length)] + '/health');
  sleep(0.2);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure tail latency under network jitter?
- How would you adapt tests when a new edge region is added?
